A particle at perpendicular distance $d$ from the axis of rotation contributes kinetic energy $T = (1)/(2) m omega^2 d^2$. In a rigid body all particles rotate with the same angular velocity, so
$
  T = (1)/(2) sum_i m_i bold(dot(x)_i)^2 = (1)/(2) I omega^2,
$
which defines the *moment of inertia* of the rigid body about that axis,
$
  I = sum_i m_i d_i^2, quad d_i = abs(bold(hat(n)) times bold(x_i)).
$
At large $N$ the particles are densely spaced and the sums are approximated by integrals against the density of mass:
$
  M = sum_i m_i = integral rho(bold(x)) dif^3 bold(x), quad I = integral rho(bold(x)) x_perp^2 dif^3 bold(x),
$
where $x_perp$ is the perpendicular distance from $bold(x)$ to the axis; typically the density is uniform, $rho(bold(x)) = rho_0$.

The bigger $I$ is, the harder it is to rotate the body: it is effectively a _rotational mass_.
